[
    {
        "content": "<p>Hi all, I am about to define certain valuation function from subset of the unit interval (therefore <code>Set &lt;| Real.Icc 0 1</code>) to real numbers. These functions are measurable functions from the unit interval to <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">R</span></span></span></span>. But as far as I studied measure theory, measures only need to be subadditive. I need them to be additive and I am new to that part of mathlib. <a href=\"http://leansearch.net\">leansearch.net</a> was of no help. What's the correct def?</p>",
        "id": 577037947,
        "sender_full_name": "Shreyas Srinivas",
        "timestamp": 1772559472
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I don't get your question. You're looking for the correct def for what?</p>",
        "id": 577039049,
        "sender_full_name": "Rémy Degenne",
        "timestamp": 1772559841
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>To get an additive measure</p>",
        "id": 577039139,
        "sender_full_name": "Shreyas Srinivas",
        "timestamp": 1772559869
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>If by additive you mean <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/MeasureTheory/Measure/MeasureSpace.html#MeasureTheory.measure_union\">MeasureTheory.measure_union</a>, then <code>Measure</code></p>",
        "id": 577039401,
        "sender_full_name": "Rémy Degenne",
        "timestamp": 1772559961
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Right apologies that’s the one. For some reason I glitched and thought measures were only subadditive.</p>",
        "id": 577039693,
        "sender_full_name": "Shreyas Srinivas",
        "timestamp": 1772560053
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/MeasureTheory/OuterMeasure/Defs.html#MeasureTheory.OuterMeasure\">MeasureTheory.OuterMeasure</a> is the subadditive one.</p>",
        "id": 577040579,
        "sender_full_name": "Rémy Degenne",
        "timestamp": 1772560349
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Is there a shorter path to a bounded measure than just writing it out explicitly as a Prop? I am guessing probability theory uses it.</p>",
        "id": 577045186,
        "sender_full_name": "Shreyas Srinivas",
        "timestamp": 1772561785
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/MeasureTheory/Measure/Typeclasses/Finite.html#MeasureTheory.IsFiniteMeasure\">MeasureTheory.IsFiniteMeasure</a> ?</p>",
        "id": 577045403,
        "sender_full_name": "Rémy Degenne",
        "timestamp": 1772561873
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>No I want to fix an explicit bound <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>:</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">b : \\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">b</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">R</span></span></span></span></p>",
        "id": 577045463,
        "sender_full_name": "Shreyas Srinivas",
        "timestamp": 1772561893
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>If the total mass is exactly 1, then <code>IsProbabilityMeasure</code>. Otherwise no.</p>",
        "id": 577045585,
        "sender_full_name": "Rémy Degenne",
        "timestamp": 1772561941
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Oh. I could work with 1, but the general theory has no restriction like that.</p>",
        "id": 577045672,
        "sender_full_name": "Shreyas Srinivas",
        "timestamp": 1772561973
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Use <code>IsFiniteMeasure µ</code> and write <code>µ univ</code> whenever you would write your <code>b</code>?</p>",
        "id": 577045823,
        "sender_full_name": "Rémy Degenne",
        "timestamp": 1772562024
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Hmm. Maybe that works. Or maybe I could just set <code>\\mu univ = b</code></p>",
        "id": 577045902,
        "sender_full_name": "Shreyas Srinivas",
        "timestamp": 1772562052
    }
]